## Not one piece at a time

GPT-2 writes text one token at a time, left to right (Ch 11). A picture could be made the same
way, one patch after another, but many image generators work differently: they start with the whole
picture as random noise and clean it up a little at a time, everywhere at once. This is a <GlossaryPopover term="diffusion">diffusion model</GlossaryPopover>.

Training works backwards from the result. Take a real picture and add a little random noise, then a
little more, step by step, until nothing of the picture is left. A network is trained to look at a
noisy picture, told how many steps of noise it has had, and guess the noise that was
added.<Cite id="sohl-dickstein2015" /> To make something new, start from pure noise and repeatedly
subtract the noise the network predicts. Each step leaves something a little more like the training
pictures.<Cite id="ho2020" />

## Try it with dots

A real image has millions of numbers. Here, each "picture" is a single dot with just two numbers,
its position, and the training pictures are dots scattered along a shape. Train the tiny network
(two layers of neurons like the ones in Ch 6) to predict the noise, then paint 400 new dots,
each starting from pure noise:

<DiffusionToy />

Each dot is drawn independently, from its own random start, yet together they land on the shape.
The network never stored the training dots; it learned which way to nudge a noisy dot to make it
more like them. Before training, the nudges are random, and painting gives a smudge.

## From dots to pictures

Real image generators do the same thing with far bigger networks and with every pixel's colors
instead of two numbers. To save work, *latent* diffusion models remove the noise from a compressed
version of the image, and they can steer each step with a text description, so the noise becomes
the picture you asked for.<Cite id="rombach2022" /> The original recipe took 1,000 small steps to
make one picture;<Cite id="ho2020" /> the toy above uses 50.

<RecapCard>

- A diffusion model learns to predict the noise that was added to a training example.
- To make something new, it starts from pure noise and removes a little predicted noise at each
  step.
- Unlike GPT-2's one-token-at-a-time writing, it refines the whole picture at every step.

</RecapCard>

**Next question:** models can now write their working before they answer. Does that make them
better?
